I'm currently running a firewall on 2.6.18 vanilla + OpenSwan KLIPS and
NATT with iptables 1.3.6. I'm having an issue where we're attempting to
pass outbound SMTP traffic to what appears to be a highly congested
hosting / filtering server (*cough* Frontbridge *cough*) . They're
sending back duplicate TCP ACKs in what I'm guessing is some sort of RFC
2581 attempt at tcp congestion control. In any case, the duplicates are
not being passed back through the firewall.

Anyone have any insight as to whether this is intentional behavior, a
bug, or me just doing something boneheaded?


Thanks,

Erik Carlseen

Guide to the sample data:
I put double asterisks (**) in front of the first four duplicate ACK 
packets lost on the internal interface. There are more in there.

[ PUBLIC IP ] - The public IP address of our SMTP server (eth0:1 on our 

                 firewall)

[SMTP SERVER] - The private IP address of our SMTP server (eth1 on our
                 firewall)

Everything else should be somewhat self-explanatory, please e-mail me if 
it's not.


